WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.760
The keypad detector
from the last lesson works,

2
00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:04.840
and to reject voices that fake a key,

3
00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:06.680
it computes every bin of every block.

4
00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:08.460
Then you put it on the cheap chip.

5
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.260
Forty-two thousand
multiplications a block,

6
00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:12.040
one point six five million a second,

7
00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:13.760
and the chip manages about a million.

8
00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:14.840
It falls behind.

9
00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:18.430
Is the DFT really doing
forty-two thousand different things?

10
00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:19.530
Take a block of eight.

11
00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:22.330
Bin one's test wheel turns back
forty-five degrees a sample,

12
00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:24.010
bin five's two hundred and twenty-five.

13
00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:25.910
On the even samples
they point the same way.

14
00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:27.910
On the odd ones, exactly opposite.

15
00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:30.910
So add up the turned even samples once:
the even part.

16
00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:33.000
Then the odd ones: the odd part.

17
00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:34.660
Bin one is even plus odd,

18
00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.000
bin five is even minus odd.

19
00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:37.360
Two bins for the price of one.

20
00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:40.250
Try a rough one thousand hertz tone: one,

21
00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:42.890
one, zero, minus one,
minus one, minus one, zero, one.

22
00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:44.700
The even part is two, pointing right.

23
00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:46.360
The odd samples walk two right and two up,

24
00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.020
but each sits one sample later,

25
00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:51.640
so turn the walk back one more step:
two point eight three, pointing right.

26
00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:55.290
Bin one is two plus two point eight three:
four point eight three.

27
00:00:55.310 --> 00:00:57.390
Bin five is two minus
two point eight three:

28
00:00:57.410 --> 00:00:58.430
minus point eight three.

29
00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:00.930
Turn, add, subtract:
that step is a butterfly,

30
00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:02.350
four multiplications.

31
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.160
Now the dramatic move.

32
00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:05.900
A four-sample DFT is a DFT,

33
00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:09.320
so split it too, and again, down to single
samples, which cost nothing.

34
00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:11.780
Only butterflies are left,
three layers of four:

35
00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:13.900
forty-eight multiplications
instead of a hundred and twenty-eight.

36
00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.440
That's the FFT,
the fast Fourier transform.

37
00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:18.480
Back to the line.

38
00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.480
The FFT halves the block again and again,

39
00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:22.380
so its length must be a power of two,

40
00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:24.680
and two hundred and five
is five times forty-one.

41
00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:26.240
Take two hundred and fifty-six:

42
00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:28.160
every keypad tone still gets its own bin,

43
00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:30.360
and the chip can spend
thirty-two thousand a block.

44
00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:33.130
Now split, and watch the bill.

45
00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.726
No splits:
a hundred and thirty-one thousand.

46
00:01:35.746 --> 00:01:36.768
One: sixty-six thousand.

47
00:01:36.788 --> 00:01:38.610
Two: thirty-four thousand, still over.

48
00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:39.830
Three, and it fits.

49
00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:42.730
All the way down, the FFT needs
four thousand and ninety-six,

50
00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:43.710
an eighth of the chip.

51
00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:46.760
Your turn: pick the block,

52
00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:48.240
add splits until the meter fits,

53
00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:49.820
and decode a caller's digits live.

54
00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:51.680
The FFT is not a rougher DFT:

55
00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:53.040
it gives exactly the same bins,

56
00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:55.880
still thirty-one point two five
hertz apart, just fast enough.

57
00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:57.810
Need only a few bins?

58
00:01:57.830 --> 00:01:59.930
Goertzel is a filter
ringing at one bin's point.

59
00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:01.070
For bin twenty of key five,

60
00:02:01.090 --> 00:02:03.130
its last two outputs give
ninety-one point eight,

61
00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:05.670
the DFT's match,
at one multiplication a sample.

62
00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:08.110
Under about sixteen bins,
it beats the FFT.

63
00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:12.090
The live line sets
its bar at twelve percent.

64
00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:13.710
For a week, everything is fine.

65
00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:15.970
Then loud fours get thrown out
as two keys at once,

66
00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:17.790
and faint rural keys go missing.

67
00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:19.950
Remember the spill
from the last lesson? It's back.

68
00:02:19.990 --> 00:02:21.010
That's the next bug.
